WEBVTT


00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.760
Now don't get all overwhelmed with
short row shaping on the double bed.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.660
It's really, if you think of it,
it's the same as on the single bed.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.700
The differences come into play with your
needle arrangement, stitch patterning,

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:20.910
needles out of work, and maybe racking,
but let's start simple and look at double

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:21.240
bed

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.200
short row shaping with one
by one short row decreases.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.189
When you're working one by one ribbing,
the garment stitch count is spread over

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:32.970
both beds.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.780
For example, in this image, I have 21
stitches in work 11 on the main bed, 10 on

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.100
the ribber, right?

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.790
This isn't the case for all variations of
full needle rib, but for right now, let's

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.210
concentrate on 1x1

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:49.710
ribbing.

00:00:51.195 --> 00:00:56.985
My instructions are to decrease five
stitches every other row X number of

00:00:56.985 --> 00:00:57.405
times.

00:00:58.754 --> 00:01:03.345
Now, if you're working on a single bed,
you'd set your carriage to hold, pull out

00:01:03.345 --> 00:01:08.265
five needles to hold position, knit two
rows, wrapping the needles to prevent

00:01:08.265 --> 00:01:08.865
holes.

00:01:09.554 --> 00:01:13.005
We're going to do just the same
thing with one by one ribbing.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:20.115
We'll start by setting both
carriages to hold both directions.

00:01:21.405 --> 00:01:25.155
Opposite the carriage, pull
five needles into hold position.

00:01:25.605 --> 00:01:27.345
That's one main bed.

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:32.865
One ribber bed, one main bed, one ribber
bed, one main bed for a total of five

00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:33.465
needles.

00:01:34.664 --> 00:01:35.955
Going to knit to the left.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.120
Notice that the main bed needle is closest
to the working yarn, so use that working

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.330
yarn and wrap that needle and knit back.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.960
Pull five needles to hold.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.210
This time it's going to be a ribber
- main - ribber -main- ribber.

00:02:03.345 --> 00:02:09.085
Knit across and this time it's the ribber
needle that's closest to the working yarn.

00:02:09.884 --> 00:02:12.555
Wrap that needle and knit back.

00:02:14.325 --> 00:02:21.555
Keep repeating, putting five needles into
hold, knit, wrap, knit, across the work.

00:02:24.925 --> 00:02:30.265
Now, just like in short rows on the main
bed, you need to adjust your weight.

00:02:31.435 --> 00:02:34.975
You're going to have to remove the
ribber comb and use weight hangers.

00:02:35.575 --> 00:02:40.675
Be sure there's even weight on your
knitting all the way across the bed, and

00:02:40.675 --> 00:02:44.075
adjust the weight as your
work in the short rows.

00:02:44.865 --> 00:02:48.085
This may be the hardest
part of this whole exercise.

00:02:48.535 --> 00:02:51.265
So pay attention to the
position of the stitches.

00:02:51.675 --> 00:02:57.675
And the formation of the stitches that are
in the needles in work as you knit, it's

00:02:57.675 --> 00:03:03.054
easy for them to pop off if the
weight isn't even across the knitting.

00:03:07.325 --> 00:03:10.375
Notice that we're holding
an odd number of stitches.

00:03:10.704 --> 00:03:16.195
The wraps are alternating from a ribber
stitch to a main bed stitch and back

00:03:16.195 --> 00:03:16.615
again.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.600
I specifically chose this so that
you could take a look at the wraps.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.350
I've blocked the heck out of this swatch.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.080
Can you see the wraps on
this side of the knitting?

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:42.310
Because of the odd number of stitches
and we wrapped ribber needle -  main bed

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:43.780
needle alternately.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.420
Wraps showing on both
sides of the knitting.

00:03:49.109 --> 00:03:54.720
Now, in most cases is probably doesn't
matter, but I want you to be aware of it.

00:03:56.350 --> 00:04:01.600
So instead of decreasing five stitches, we
decreased maybe four or an even number of

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:02.290
stitches.

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:05.480
The wraps would be all on the same bed.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.075
So you might want to determine which
side of your knitting is going to be the

00:04:12.075 --> 00:04:19.394
private side and which side is the public
side in relation to where the wraps lay

00:04:19.815 --> 00:04:21.015
and how visible they are.

00:04:24.075 --> 00:04:29.175
On the other hand, you may just want to
let the chips (or the wraps) fall where they

00:04:29.175 --> 00:04:31.455
lay and not worry about it.
